{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1120","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1120","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1120\",\"parent_revision\":\"0df26e9563aef77d44a73786f229d886a325f6bf078209f28ea14e38921bda84\",\"root_revision\":\"0df26e9563aef77d44a73786f229d886a325f6bf078209f28ea14e38921bda84\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: 2026-04-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n10 `async def` test methods in `plugins/frustration-analyzer/tests/test_extract_user_messages.py` have no `@pytest.mark.asyncio` decorator and no `asyncio_mode = \\\"auto\\\"` configuration. Pytest collects them as coroutine objects and reports PASSED without executing \u2014 zero coverage behind a green CI signal.\\n\\nAffected classes: `TestExtractUserMessagesSingleBatch`, `TestExtractUserMessagesMultiBatch`, `TestExtractUserMessagesFiltering` (lines 82, 94, 109, 125, 138, 152, 169, 189, 205, 233).\\n\\nFix: verify `asyncio_mode = \\\"auto\\\"` in the plugin's `pyproject.toml [tool.pytest.ini_options]`, or add `@pytest.mark.asyncio` to all 10 methods. Verify by running `pytest -v plugins/frustration-analyzer/tests/test_extract_user_messages.py` and confirming each test shows PASSED (not silently skipped).\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-03-29T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **fix silent async tests in frustration-analyzer test_extract_user_messages.py** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-03-29T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Test quality audit 2026-03-29\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-03-29\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-03-30)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Navigate to the repository root: `/home/user/claude_skills`\\n2. Run pytest targeting only the affected test file:\\n   ```bash\\n   cd plugins/frustration-analyzer/tests && python -m pytest test_extract_user_messages.py -v 2>&1 | grep -E 'PASSED|FAILED|ERROR|WARNING'\\n   ```\\n3. Observe: all 10 `async def` test methods report `PASSED` in the output with zero actual execution \u2014 pytest collects them as coroutine objects and marks them passed without awaiting.\\n4. To confirm zero execution, add a deliberate failure inside any one `async def` test (e.g., `assert False`) and rerun \u2014 it will still report `PASSED`, proving the coroutine was never called.\\n\\n### Output / Evidence\\n\\n- Running `pytest test_extract_user_messages.py -v` reports 10 PASSed tests in `TestExtractUserMessagesSingleBatch`, `TestExtractUserMessagesMultiBatch`, and `TestExtractUserMessagesFiltering` with no actual async execution.\\n- Running `pytest test_extract_user_messages.py -v --co` (collect-only) shows those tests as coroutine objects rather than properly scheduled async tests.\\n- No `pyproject.toml` exists anywhere under `plugins/frustration-analyzer/` \u2014 confirmed via Glob search. The plugin has no `[tool.pytest.ini_options]` section and no `asyncio_mode = 'auto'` config.\\n- The root `pyproject.toml` at `/home/user/claude_skills/pyproject.toml` line 81 sets `asyncio_mode = \\\"auto\\\"` but this is NOT inherited when tests are run from the plugin subdirectory.\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n8/10 \u2014 Tests report green CI signal with zero real coverage. The 10 affected methods cover the primary public function (`extract_user_messages`) across all three test classes. Any regression in that function is undetectable.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: Reliable CI coverage signal for `extract_user_messages` in the frustration-analyzer plugin\\n- Bottleneck: The entire `TestExtractUserMessagesSingleBatch`, `TestExtractUserMessagesMultiBatch`, and `TestExtractUserMessagesFiltering` suites (10 test methods) are dead code masquerading as passing tests\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- `extract_user_messages` gains actual test coverage for single-batch output, multi-batch splitting, token budgets, and message filtering\\n- CI will correctly fail when regressions are introduced in `extract_user_messages`\\n- Eliminates false confidence from the current green signal\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nEach `async def` test method in the affected classes executes its body fully: the `await extract_user_messages(...)` call runs, assertions evaluate against real output, and failures propagate to pytest as test failures.\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\nAll 10 async test methods in `plugins/frustration-analyzer/tests/test_extract_user_messages.py` are scheduled and awaited by pytest. Either:\\n- A `pyproject.toml` exists at `plugins/frustration-analyzer/` with `[tool.pytest.ini_options]` containing `asyncio_mode = \\\"auto\\\"` and `pytest-asyncio` declared as a test dependency, OR\\n- Each of the 10 `async def` test methods carries `@pytest.mark.asyncio` decorator\\n\\nThe preferred approach (consistent with root `pyproject.toml` and `plugins/development-harness/pyproject.toml`) is creating a plugin-level `pyproject.toml` with `asyncio_mode = \\\"auto\\\"`.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. Running `cd plugins/frustration-analyzer/tests && python -m pytest test_extract_user_messages.py -v` shows all 10 previously-async tests executing their bodies (not silently passing as coroutine objects).\\n2. Inserting `assert False` into any one of the 10 async test methods causes that test to report `FAILED` \u2014 not `PASSED`.\\n3. Running `python -m pytest test_extract_user_messages.py -v` with no forced failures shows all 10 tests as `PASSED` with real assertions validated.\\n4. `pytest-asyncio` is declared as a test dependency in `plugins/frustration-analyzer/pyproject.toml` (or the 10 decorators are present), verifiable by reading the file.\\n5. Existing sync tests (`TestCountTokens`, `TestEncoderCache`, `TestDefaultBatchTokens`) continue to pass unchanged.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Prior work | `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/frustration-analyzer/tests/test_extract_user_messages.py` \u2014 10 affected async methods at lines 82, 94, 109, 125, 138, 152, 169, 189, 205, 233 |\\n| Prior work | `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/frustration-analyzer/tests/conftest.py` \u2014 no asyncio_mode config present |\\n| Prior work | `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/pyproject.toml` \u2014 reference pattern: asyncio_mode = \\\"auto\\\" at line 30 |\\n| Prior work | `/home/user/claude_skills/pyproject.toml` \u2014 root asyncio_mode = \\\"auto\\\" at line 81 |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 self-contained test configuration fix\\n- Blocks: Accurate CI coverage reporting for frustration-analyzer plugin\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 The fix is adding a `pyproject.toml` with `[tool.pytest.ini_options]` and `asyncio_mode = \\\"auto\\\"` to `plugins/frustration-analyzer/`, or decorating 10 methods. Reference pattern exists in `plugins/development-harness/pyproject.toml`. No logic changes required.\\n\",\"digest\":\"28b75637ec8212ab0108d123c38d611075ff8ce05afc8a6beafc5bea15cba138\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCt7hQ\"}"}